rai:dataSocialImpact:
PitchBench aims to support more transparent, reproducible, and diagnostically precise evaluation of audio-language models.

Positive impacts include:
- enabling controlled analysis of pitch-perception capabilities that are often obscured in broader music-understanding benchmarks;
- encouraging interpretable evaluation methodologies that isolate specific auditory variables and perceptual failure modes;
- supporting reproducible research through deterministic stimulus generation and released generation code;
- enabling researchers to generate new evaluation distributions without relying exclusively on a fixed static benchmark;
- and providing an extensible framework for controlled studies of auditory perception in AI systems.

Potential risks include:
- benchmark contamination if official held-out configurations become publicly replicated;
- overfitting if models are trained directly on released benchmark instances;
- overgeneralization of benchmark performance to broader musical competence or real-world auditory reasoning;
- and misuse of benchmark scores as proxies for deployment readiness in production music systems.

To mitigate these risks, the benchmark documentation explicitly distinguishes controlled auditory evaluation from general music understanding, and the official held-out benchmark configurations are partially withheld to preserve long-term benchmark integrity.
